meta {
  name: Concurrency Guard (409)
  type: http
  seq: 7
}

put {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/document/{{documentId}}
  body: multipartForm
  auth: inherit
}

body:multipart-form {
  metadata: @file(../payloads/update-metadata-only.json)
}

settings {
  encodeUrl: true
  timeout: 0
}

tests {
  test("succeeds on its own (guard only triggers under real concurrency)", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(200);
  });
}

docs {
  `updateDocument` uses an atomic Mongo `findAndModify` keyed on the document's *current*
  version (compare-and-swap): only the first of several concurrent updates against the
  same version succeeds; the rest get 409 Conflict with detail "Document ... was
  concurrently modified by another request - reload and retry".

  Bruno has no built-in way to fire truly simultaneous requests from the GUI, so clicking
  "Send" here just performs a normal single update (200). To actually exercise the guard,
  fire a burst of simultaneous requests from a shell instead, e.g. from this `bruno/`
  directory:

    for i in $(seq 1 10); do
      curl -s -o /tmp/burst_$i.json -w "%{http_code}\n" -X PUT {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/document/{{documentId}} \
        -F "metadata={\"metadata\":{\"burst\":\"$i\"}};type=application/json" &
    done
    wait

  Expect exactly one `200` and the rest `409` - confirmed during development: 1 success,
  9 conflicts, and no lost updates or double version bumps.
}
